// SenderKeyDistribution gives the receiver the sender's current sender key
// for the chat, so it can decrypt the sender's SENDER_KEY_MESSAGE envelopes
// there. This is how a group message is encrypted once and read by every
// member: each member device first distributes its sender key to every other
// member device pairwise, under their Double Ratchet sessions, then encrypts
// group messages under it. The distribution is per (sending device, chat).
//
// Sender keys are for groups only; a DM is always pairwise. libsignal keys
// a sender key by a UUID distribution id the sender chooses per group, and
// a group's common.v1.ChatId is a 16-byte UUID, so clients use the chat id
// itself as the distribution id. A "new key" below is a fresh sender key
// state under that same distribution id, which yields a new chain id.
//
// A distribution is addressed to users, never to a subset of a user's
// devices: Mailbox.SendEnvelopes holds every send to a named user's full
// device set, so a member whose one new device lacks the key is sent it on
// all of their devices, and the others discard the repeat. A sender
// distributes:
//   - to every member before its first group send, and, for a device that
//     has just joined, at once on joining, whether or not it means to
//     speak: that distribution is what opens its pairwise sessions with
//     every member device, and every member device replies with its own
//     distribution INSIDE the session so opened, as an ordinary CIPHERTEXT,
//     rather than fetching the joiner's prekey bundle. A PQXDH initial
//     message gives its recipient a working session at once, so the reply
//     needs no bundle; fetching one anyway would spend a one-time prekey
//     of the joiner's per member device, up to the whole pool on one join.
//     A member fetches the joiner's bundle only if it must send before the
//     joiner's distribution has arrived;
//   - to a new member once a roster change reveals them, and to a member
//     again once a DeviceMismatch reveals a device of theirs it has not
//     reached;
//   - to every remaining member, with a NEW key, after a member leaves,
//     before its next group send, so the leaver cannot read what follows;
//   - to every member, with a NEW key, once the current key is older than
//     a maximum age the client fixes (Signal rotates after 14 days), so a
//     key that leaks does not expose the group's traffic indefinitely;
//   - on request, when a DecryptionErrorMessage says a member could not
//     decrypt a sender key message.
//
// Ordering: a recipient must hold the distribution before the first
// SENDER_KEY_MESSAGE under it, or decryption fails with no sender key state
// and the recipient falls back to a DecryptionErrorMessage. A mailbox
// orders envelopes by the server's acceptance, so a sender waits for OK
// from the distribution's SendEnvelopes call before making the group send.
// It does not wait for delivery receipts.
//
